# The Boy Who Grew in the Dark

**Chapter One: The Bell That Never Rings**

The bell at St. Aldric's Academy was a brass thing that hung in a tower of grey stone, and it rang at exactly 8:02 every morning. For seventeen years, it had been the most reliable thing in the world.

Except for Elias.

Elias Vane was seventeen, and he was a wood elf.

He had been born in the old pines behind the village of Fernhollow, where the trees were so old their roots had cracked the ground open and the moss grew in patterns that looked, to anyone who knew how to read them, like the names of things that had died. His mother had been a keeper of the grove. His father had been a man who had left, and whose leaving was the only thing Elias had ever been able to understand.

When he was six, his mother had carried him to the edge of the road and said, *"You are not like us, Eli. You will not fit. So you will go where the light is, and you will learn to be quiet about it."*

So he had.

He had gone to Fernhollow, and then to the town of Marrow, and then, at the age of eleven, he had walked into the admissions office of St. Aldric's Academy and said, *"I'd like to apply."*

The headmaster had looked at him for a long time. Then he had said, *"Very well. But you will not be told. And you will not be told again."*

That was the deal. Elias would be a student at St. Aldric's. He would sit in the same classrooms, eat the same bread, and befriend the same people as everyone else. And in exchange, he would never once let anyone find out that he was a wood elf.

The trouble was that being a wood elf was not a secret you could keep by simply not saying anything. It was a secret that lived in your body.

It lived in the way his ears grew, slowly, over the course of a single afternoon, until they were long and pointed and could hear a pencil scratch across a desk three rooms away.

It lived in the way his skin turned the colour of bark in the first week of autumn, and then, in the second week, the colour of bark in the spring.

It lived in the way he could not sleep in a bed. He slept in the dark, and he slept with his back to a wall, and he slept with his hands folded over his knees, and he slept with his eyes open for the first hour of the night, listening.

And it lived in the way he could not, ever, be in a room with a fire.

---

**Chapter Two: The Room With No Fire**

Elias's room at St. Aldric's was the smallest in the building, and it was on the fourth floor, and it had a window that faced the courtyard.

The courtyard was where the school kept its fire.

Every evening, at six o'clock, the caretaker, Mr. Halloway, would light the great brazier in the middle of the courtyard, and the whole school would gather around it. It was a tradition. It was a fire. It was warm. It was, in Elias's opinion, the single most dangerous thing in the building.

He had learned to manage it. He had learned to stand at the edge of the crowd, with his back to the wall of the building, and to pretend to be interested in the conversation. He had learned to hold his breath when the heat rose. He had learned to walk away before the fire had a chance to notice him.

But he had never once been able to sit near it.

One evening, in the second week of October, the fire had been lit, and the courtyard was full of students, and Mr. Halloway was telling a story about a boy who had once tried to steal a dragon's egg, and everyone was laughing.

Elias stood at the edge of the crowd, and he watched the flames, and he felt the heat rise off them, and he felt the pull of them, and he felt the old, familiar ache in his chest.

He had to leave.

He had to leave, and he had to leave without anyone noticing.

So he did. He turned, and he walked, and he walked past the fire, and he walked past the laughing, and he walked past the brazier, and he walked into the dark of the courtyard, and he walked up the stairs, and he walked into his room, and he closed the door, and he sat on the floor, and he put his back against the wall, and he waited for the fire to go out.

It did not go out.

It never went out.

And so he waited, and he waited, and he waited, until the last of the students had gone home, and until the courtyard was empty, and until the fire had burned down to a single ember, and until the ember had gone out.

And then, at last, he let himself sleep.

---

**Chapter Three: The Girl Who Could Not Stop Talking**

Elias's best friend at St. Aldric's was a girl named Mara.

Mara was a human, and she was loud, and she was kind, and she was the only person in the world who had ever made him laugh out loud in a classroom.

She was also, in Elias's opinion, the most dangerous person he had ever met.

Mara was a talker. She talked about everything. She talked about her family, and her teachers, and her dreams, and the things she had read in the library, and the things she had seen in the courtyard. She talked about the fire. She talked about the fire constantly.

And she talked about the fire in a way that made Elias's ears twitch.

One afternoon, in the third week of October, Mara had been sitting next to him in the library, and she had been telling him about a book she had found in the stacks, and she had said, *"You know, Elias, I think the fire is a bit of a cliché. Everyone talks about the fire. Nobody talks about the trees."*

Elias had looked at her. He had looked at her for a long time.

And then he had said, *"Trees?"*

And she had said, *"Yes. Trees. I think the trees are the real story. I think the fire is just the thing people use to talk about the trees."*

Elias had said nothing.

He had said nothing, and he had looked at her, and he had looked at her, and he had looked at her, and he had said nothing.

And then, at last, he had said, *"That's a very interesting thing to say, Mara."*

And she had said, *"I know. I've been thinking about it a lot lately."*

And then she had said, *"You know what I think it means? I think the fire is a way of saying that we're all part of something bigger than ourselves. That we're all part of the same thing."*

Elias had said nothing.

He had said nothing, and he had looked at her, and he had looked at her, and he had looked at her, and he had said nothing.

And then, at last, he had said, *"That's a very interesting thing to say, Mara."*

